<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naletale</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naletale is an archaeological site in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> with an elaborately decorated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> enclosure. It is one of the stone-walled centres, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danangombe">Danangombe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a>, associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a>. Such sites served as administrative, ceremonial and residential centres for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changamire">Changamire</a> court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naletale.jpg" alt="Naletale" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fanny Schertzer · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naletale_Ruins_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
